    "growth_rate_source": "judgment: organic revenue growth (FY2025 +5.5% reported, FY2026 guide ~2% reported / 2-3% organic, Q2-26 orders +42%)",
    "growth_divergence_warning": "Equity CAGR (31% 5yr, 49% 3yr) diverges far more than 30% from revenue and EPS CAGRs because the May-2023 Evoqua acquisition was paid in stock (~$7bn of equity issued, shares 180M -> 242M). Every historical CAGR is acquisition-inflated; none is a forward organic rate. Selected 5.0% organic instead.",
    "notes": "FCF CAGRs on the owner-FCF series (reported FCF less SBC; interest income not deducted as Xylem is net debt). Owner FCF: FY2020 615, FY2022 351, FY2025 842 (FY2025 owner basis includes $15M equity-award withholding; earlier years SBC only). The 3yr FCF CAGR starts from a depressed FY2022 (supply-chain working-capital build) and overstates trend. SBC-adjusted and reported FCF CAGRs are close: SBC is small (~0.6-0.8% of revenue) and not outgrowing cash flow."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Xylem delivers its FY2026 guide (~$9.2bn revenue, adj. EBITDA margin 23.1-23.5%) and converts the record Q2-26 backlog (orders +42%) into mid-single-digit organic growth through FY2028, carried by non-discretionary US municipal replacement, PFAS treatment upgrades and data-center cooling/water reuse. The margin program (price, productivity, divesting the dilutive international metering business) lifts cash conversion steadily as restructuring cash fades. Growth decays to GDP-plus by year 10.",
    "bull": "Data-center, semiconductor and life-sciences water demand becomes a durable second engine rather than a cyclical spike, PFAS deadlines drive a multi-year treatment capex wave, and Xylem's unique pumps-treatment-metering-analytics breadth wins system-level contracts. Organic growth runs 7-8% for three years and portfolio simplification plus software attach (Xylem Vue) push margins ~150bp above base.",
    "bear": "The order surge is AI-capex pull-forward that fades with a hyperscaler slowdown; FY2026's trimmed revenue guide proves the leading indicator, with gains margin-only. Municipal funding slips, Applied Water tracks a weak industrial cycle, and a dense M&A calendar (TriOS, WaterFleet, Cornell/Roper on top of Evoqua) under a new CFO soaks up management bandwidth and rising leverage, leaving margins flat and growth at 2-3%."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "Value Xylem on owner FCF from a component build calibrated to the cash it actually converts, not to adjusted EBITDA. The company's adjusted figures exclude ~2pp of recurring restructuring and special charges that have been cash every year since the Evoqua deal; capitalizing adjusted EBITDA would overstate the business. Acquisitions are treated as NPV-neutral and excluded from both flows and bridge.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Cash conversion: if restructuring/special cash charges do not fade, the 250bp margin ramp in base does not happen",
      "Data-center order durability (Q2-26 orders +42% vs revenue +2%)",
      "M&A integration and leverage: Cornell/Roper ($1.46bn) not yet in net debt; if overpaid, value falls by the excess",
      "D&A glide: falling acquired-intangible amortization raises cash tax over the horizon",
      "Terminal value share is high for a 9% WACC / 3% growth build; the 18x cap is close to binding"
    ]
  },
